  "query_preview": "..\n\nMinimal adjustments to note regarding the synoptic pattern today as \nbroad ridging persists with quick northwest flow aloft. Geopotential \nheights rise slightly over Southeast Michigan as the longwave axis \nbecomes reestablished further west, closer to The Rockies. \nPositioned downstream of the apex, mid-level geopotential heights \nover Lower Michigan appear largely unchanged, if not slightly \nhigher, while lower column subsidence offer confidence in mainly dry \nconditions. As has been the case for most of the week, near surface \nmoisture remains pervasive, marked by dewpoints in 60s. Surface-\nbased instability faces a challenging unlock process warmer mid-\nlevels translate into poor lapse rates of less than 6 C/km. Latest \nNWP solutions indicate meager MUCAPE on the order of 250-500 J/kg. \nWeak CVA activated by perturbed flow aloft is not expected to \nsuffice in triggering any meaningful convection, although a few \nisolated narrow-diameter showers cannot completely be ruled out, \nespecially with any favorable lake breeze interactions. As for the \nfate of upstream MCS activity moving from The Plains into portions \nof the Midwest, low-end potential exists for a glancing pass at the \nsouthern portion of the CWA this evening or early tonight. Should \nthis materialize, it would mainly affect locations south of I-94 as \nevidenced by the slower and more eastward trajectory of the 13.05Z \nHRRR, compared to the 00Z run.\n\nLow convective potential again Friday as flow weakens aloft and the \nridge gets flattened by a digging Canadian shortwave. Similar \ntemperature trend as 850 mb temperatures linger in the mid teens, \nresulting in highs around the mid 80s. Surface high pressure over \nOntario will have a weak hold on gradient flow locally, becoming \nnortherly composed. Precipitation chances will be dictated by \nupstream convective evolution, mainly along the same Ohio border \nvicinity zone. A moist low-level jet approaches Saturday night along \na warm front leading to a sharp Theta-E surge as low pressure lifts \nin from the southwest. Potential exists for organized convection \nwith the initial front, and again with a trailing cold front through \nSunday. Winds shift from south to northwest behind the front with a \nbrief reprieve in temperatures and dewpoints for Monday and Tuesday.",
